h-academy-id "q-16196" :question/problem "Determine the graph of the following parametrically defined curve:\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to create a table of values and then sketch the curve. First, we work out the $x$-values using the formula $x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$ This gives\n\n| **$\\theta $** | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ | $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ | $\\pi $ | $\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ | $\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $2$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $1$ |\n| $y$ | $$ | $$ | $$ | $$ | $$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nNow, we work out the $y$ values using $y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$ This gives\n\n| **$\\theta $** | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ | $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ | $\\pi $ | $\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ | $\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $2$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $1$ |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ | $0$ | $-\\sqrt{3}$ | $-\\sqrt{3}$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nPlotting these points, we obtain the following curve:\n\n![](images/47/47615688bf550384de29a5e7e480bdab699b8eee994e5e28052e4d043e2db503.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106068 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105895 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fea43b6-fd7b-5edc-b6b7-7f8b2cbab502" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Type of Image Formed by a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9bb34d14-4e6b-5921-bbae-0804ad06b4f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102295} {:answer/id #uuid "b2b33753-c1ca-54f2-b9c6-98ba27f6fa8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A rectangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102296} {:answer/id #uuid "cb1ac350-98a0-5f6c-8490-7f08a97c5de0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102297} {:answer/id #uuid "1338e2d6-476a-5cc4-a9f7-69b9fb7f0f6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102298} {:answer/id #uuid "39e1c3d2-cf9c-5c0f-ac61-180359ad5ac6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A point, different from the origin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102299}] :answer-field/id #uuid "98d6bd43-1322-57b8-8f87-0e031cdaba61" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102294} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e72b8eba-d97d-557f-a8cb-b177b5a694e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401317} {:answer/id #uuid "239808c3-6662-5144-b216-bf28876d8c30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A rectangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401318} {:answer/id #uuid "d95c8151-cc29-5467-9bd0-4f7145939834" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401319} {:answer/id #uuid "a539e88c-cfb9-5231-b8f6-481fa85848fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401320} {:answer/id #uuid "ace8a67a-e74f-5f27-9582-9a2b3428a829" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A point, different from the origin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401321}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e72b8eba-d97d-557f-a8cb-b177b5a694e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401317} :answer-field/id #uuid "3dd41be4-e0dd-5cb0-830a-bc68816c31ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401316}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ba678a53-c013-5d44-b391-2d5cc0a9cc96" :question/math-academy-id "q-162150" :question/problem "![](images/e2/e2ab6906100a96a123b4b1520613f1e38fe2c7e77af390a8efe0596a2861727d.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nSince $T$ is the zero matrix, the image of our object under the action of $T$ is the origin."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281389 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105899 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f78fe749-e37e-53f2-879f-4c36931c1d6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Segment"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34dc3d85-bf7d-5a32-bc17-8ac750c355ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279049} {:answer/id #uuid "642b22ea-1596-5521-b3ff-b9cef628875f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279502} {:answer/id #uuid "846472d7-37b6-51a7-af37-dc5316fa1feb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279779} {:answer/id #uuid "9500f03a-d333-5927-bed1-97f253541b9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279916} {:answer/id #uuid "dbb56e0e-2818-55a9-ad7f-03187608868a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280532}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dbb56e0e-2818-55a9-ad7f-03187608868a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280532} :answer-field/id #uuid "678b25a4-44dd-597e-88cf-490c50666c6d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281013}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d31dc5a7-37f0-52db-ac08-e744b2aa0398" :question/math-academy-id "q-162161" :question/problem "The equation below describes all the points on the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ},$ where $P(3,0,-2)$ and $Q(1,1,-1).$\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ b \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in [c,1]\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\nr=p+tv\\,⟹\\,r=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand the vector equation of the  line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ is\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=3+1+0=4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281390 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105899 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f78fe749-e37e-53f2-879f-4c36931c1d6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Segment"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e7abf52-260b-5871-877d-d3cd9af93b7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278723} {:answer/id #uuid "4b3a8c37-ac70-5d4e-8764-2be1866f7061" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279265} {:answer/id #uuid "7d8754e9-d561-500f-ae97-638bb9ed4546" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279715} {:answer/id #uuid "8c01281f-bb41-5e46-8e01-ab1d074c18dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279838} {:answer/id #uuid "a1ae0f7a-ba36-5566-b7b7-82bb318a9f44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280028}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d8754e9-d561-500f-ae97-638bb9ed4546" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279715} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f5aec32-55d7-56a5-a482-291347058259" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280942}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c6ef52c7-4e9c-5db7-b87c-ad4749b7588e" :question/math-academy-id "q-162165" :question/problem "The equation below describes all the points on the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ},$ where $P(0,-2,2)$ and $Q(1,-1,1).$\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ a\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ b \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in [c,1]\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\nr=p+tv\\,⟹\\,r=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand the vector equation of the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ is\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=2+1+0=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281391 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105899 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f78fe749-e37e-53f2-879f-4c36931c1d6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Segment"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0acdb20a-bd99-5fd1-aefb-90dba9fa2833" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278696} {:answer/id #uuid "93028034-a984-5264-8d67-bb78f8f934a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279898} {:answer/id #uuid "ad375a48-0f06-5f5d-a1d5-0f4984254409" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280125} {:answer/id #uuid "d9bc1ba0-2626-5083-a821-a634e14afb68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280515} {:answer/id #uuid "f8257693-9258-5a14-bd35-cec23355b615" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280770}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8257693-9258-5a14-bd35-cec23355b615" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280770} :answer-field/id #uuid "3582b34d-fad6-5c20-b7b8-eaa6ab83ca80" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280928}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6982b399-45f0-5a83-aeee-4f0df799362d" :question/math-academy-id "q-162167" :question/problem "The equation below describes all the points on the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ},$ where $P(-3,5,0)$ and $Q(0,-3,2).$\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -8 \\\\ b\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in [c,1]\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\nr=p+tv\\,⟹\\,r=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand the vector equation of the  line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ is\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=(-3)+2+0=-1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372000 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179773 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1577f765-f98c-5020-8e45-8da2c5f092e6" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Area Scale Factor of an Affine Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41c17750-e31e-532b-ae8d-7979de4d8d78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372002} {:answer/id #uuid "ad0c1dd5-b9ea-567c-a97b-f4096b3e1a5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372003} {:answer/id #uuid "5e0e426b-b2e9-58a1-91ce-578d1bd6cc16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372004} {:answer/id #uuid "1a41d7c7-bfb3-558c-bcd5-8d233c807fdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372005} {:answer/id #uuid "681b6932-d634-563f-b59e-a68a14cc53c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372006}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "681b6932-d634-563f-b59e-a68a14cc53c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372006} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2e7db5e-75b3-5228-9ad8-cef0891cba6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372001}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d122e060-ffdf-5149-8bb7-a8a052fb44e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-162172" :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWhat is the area scale factor of the transformation?" :question/worked-solution "The matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}|=2\\cdot (-1)-3\\cdot 1=-5.\n$$\n\nSo, the area scale factor of the transformation is\n\n$$\n|det(A)|=|-5|=5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372007 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179773 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1577f765-f98c-5020-8e45-8da2c5f092e6" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Area Scale Factor of an Affine Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4dd51353-621e-56ae-bd7e-c1dcf4750e8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372009} {:answer/id #uuid "4692f893-08bf-51dc-8c1d-be1f128421c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372010} {:answer/id #uuid "dc922160-ef0e-51c1-bb3d-1ca4475e6dc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372011} {:answer/id #uuid "b3632d7d-0532-52f8-932d-ff5cdd002973" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372012} {:answer/id #uuid "2a9caf61-79c1-50ad-a6f3-d6e5f8d0d815" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372013}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4dd51353-621e-56ae-bd7e-c1dcf4750e8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372009} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab22a8ca-8854-500e-8a30-6e7d2bafc9e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372008}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7d4e1db4-c725-5d09-875d-b4ebdbd932b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-162175" :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWhat is the area scale factor of the transformation?" :question/worked-solution "The matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|=1\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 1=3.\n$$\n\nSo, the area scale factor of the transformation is\n\n$$\n|det(A)|=|3|=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389250 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179775 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e551b65-4116-58bc-b2bc-e536930c69b4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Types of Images Formed by an Affine Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df95a0cf-0d57-5a93-ac2f-e7e698ff77a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parallelogram" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389252} {:answer/id #uuid "b92ebcbf-f068-555d-863e-04c41a13ff18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389253} {:answer/id #uuid "1f7c5c2f-8c10-5091-a460-a5b671bf8c82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389254} {:answer/id #uuid "5ef4fb02-e604-5dc5-8e46-a8a75eadac14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A pentagon" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389255} {:answer/id #uuid "955f1935-0944-5d9f-9475-dcae022a4eeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389256}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df95a0cf-0d57-5a93-ac2f-e7e698ff77a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parallelogram" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389252} :answer-field/id #uuid "e581555a-cb1e-51f4-adbf-43ba3b75f28d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389251}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "11c11bf3-c97a-56d9-961f-42e1efebf284" :question/math-academy-id "q-162197" :question/problem "![](images/c3/c36f3eb07b45eebf7f4de2a26cb366ca4ab785b5c7e8e6d25eb7bbad2c418838.png)\n\nConsider the affine transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nx=3u-v-1,\\,y=2u+5v.\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the rectangle shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any affine transformation \n\n$$\nT:u⟶Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $u$ and $b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., when $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., when $det(A)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nThe given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3u-v-1 \\\\ 2u+5v\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3u-v \\\\ 2u+5v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}].$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot 5-2\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =17 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a non-singular matrix. \n\nTherefore, $T$ is invertible, and the image of the rectangle under the action of $T$ is a parallelogram."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281406 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105895 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fea43b6-fd7b-5edc-b6b7-7f8b2cbab502" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Type of Image Formed by a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a6e4373-347b-586f-904c-be59818f4945" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279110} {:answer/id #uuid "5d56f302-e7a0-5f70-b0e0-272d1328de15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A pentagon" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279448} {:answer/id #uuid "dbf17246-a02f-5adb-adca-449dd47e93a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A point, different from the origin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280536} {:answer/id #uuid "e92272a3-0f88-52d3-9b2f-9e2c60c5273f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A rectangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280642} {:answer/id #uuid "f3652a9c-788f-5fa8-a9ee-b638c4ee6bba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280719}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a6e4373-347b-586f-904c-be59818f4945" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279110} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4f360b7-8b35-5cc3-a7cb-82ef205491ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281270}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0a4d15c5-f7e2-5db8-ae64-0fe650e3a234" :question/math-academy-id "q-162199" :question/problem "![](images/eb/eb1247ddcaaf12a342aa036550ed4f9f7daaeb6037e7ebbab0de619bb6975dbe.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the pentagon shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nSince $T$ is the zero matrix, the image of our object under the action of $T$ is the origin."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106069 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105895 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fea43b6-fd7b-5edc-b6b7-7f8b2cbab502" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Type of Image Formed by a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "148840cb-8cea-5745-9f92-680dd7f0ea95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278774} {:answer/id #uuid "5bb3d88b-4028-5ec9-80c5-a2ea9fc98832" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A point, different from the origin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279438} {:answer/id #uuid "aa7deda2-0f82-5af4-aed7-d429a9518b5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280104} {:answer/id #uuid "b4822b66-d6bb-592a-a36a-a168beb56876" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280182} {:answer/id #uuid "b8ad46d1-cb7b-5e5e-844d-6c83af425320" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A quadrilateral" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280215}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8ad46d1-cb7b-5e5e-844d-6c83af425320" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A quadrilateral" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280215} :answer-field/id #uuid "47cd30e7-fd7a-5e03-89d2-7a44f08b81a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280953}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0f03e3e5-8f9e-5206-8323-aab2c0298778" :question/math-academy-id "q-162206" :question/problem "![](images/e6/e6d0ce147aec90c3840a893e36596fbadf981ff96559f65d055f4486406cca7f.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the quadrilateral shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-1\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a nonsingular matrix. Therefore, the image of our object under the action of  $T$ is a quadrilateral."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389257 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179775 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e551b65-4116-58bc-b2bc-e536930c69b4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Types of Images Formed by an Affine Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e04b870d-e25b-5d96-b606-28a110148002" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A square" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389259} {:answer/id #uuid "062427a9-0f48-53fc-94f1-c6c3d5e866d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389260} {:answer/id #uuid "b7b5ad5a-830d-59fb-be20-4b48087915f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389261} {:answer/id #uuid "4003098a-783e-5e93-bb25-5f94f36652a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A pentagon" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389262} {:answer/id #uuid "1f7e5e40-3f3f-5c2d-abac-4a9137d31956" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389263}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4003098a-783e-5e93-bb25-5f94f36652a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A pentagon" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389262} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1250926-eea3-55ee-b530-0094a4347a99" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389258}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b31d6cdc-6f82-507f-838b-9eddf7b3f11a" :question/math-academy-id "q-162215" :question/problem "![](images/1f/1f550963f047a3860566564abc2cb1e9f3939125895912762ff751767ca55c57.png)\n\nConsider the affine transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nx=2u-v,\\,y=4u-v+2.\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the pentagon shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any affine transformation \n\n$$\nT:u⟶Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $u$ and $b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., when $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., when $det(A)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nThe given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2u-v \\\\ 4u-v+2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2u-v \\\\ 4u-v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)-4\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a non-singular matrix. \n\nTherefore, $T$ is invertible, and the image of the pentagon under the action of $T$ is a pentagon."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389270 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171914 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c2c755c-59e0-5048-b20c-507f13fce441" :knowledge-point/title "Geometric Interpretation of the Roots of Unity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25fb5ca3-d3d8-556c-bfbd-18a27abab0d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c7/c70d25ac3a5ddb2d6765ef8a22f5e9774c9be06dd909d7079c3a361157881bb5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389272} {:answer/id #uuid "ddc77107-0356-5a3b-87bc-0fc7ca635b61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/91/9111f21143412d791b0195722da4f6d461444f386efd9baf609c70a4fe709fd8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389273} {:answer/id #uuid "34e1b08c-8354-55c3-a97a-4c32cfaf6d5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ba/bae536e1ec12168c413a6387f3106512173a34d92daae9ad6218040e7336c7ad.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389274} {:answer/id #uuid "fa39b0ed-b99c-5c6b-b5b4-628e084b1f3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/92/923d1a1b87833551ae6c451f15a6b5d8bdd75623e8d5c20e86357894bc24683d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389275} {:answer/id #uuid "213fcb6f-5b6e-56c4-9475-6d78a1c3d554" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e1/e11d63b3127cf3a162223ae7fca5e875e02622ec21480edd3fbc6cdddb039eae.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389276}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25fb5ca3-d3d8-556c-bfbd-18a27abab0d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c7/c70d25ac3a5ddb2d6765ef8a22f5e9774c9be06dd909d7079c3a361157881bb5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389272} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce62dc5a-db8c-574f-9cb2-3491413a4d60" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389271}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d172d3ec-37f8-5fb9-8ea7-85c7f30ed514" :question/math-academy-id "q-162239" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams shows all the $3$rd roots of unity?" :question/worked-solution "Geometrically, the $3$rd roots of unity have the following properties:\n\n- all of them lie on the unit circle centered in the origin,\n\n- one of the roots is $1,$ corresponding to the point $(1,0)$ on the complex plane,\n\n- the roots form an equilateral triangle (regular $3$-sided polygon).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n![](images/c7/c70d25ac3a5ddb2d6765ef8a22f5e9774c9be06dd909d7079c3a361157881bb5.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389277 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171914 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c2c755c-59e0-5048-b20c-507f13fce441" :knowledge-point/title "Geometric Interpretation of the Roots of Unity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d450233-f778-5146-bb61-500a2a3edb67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/82/82682434de1fe7e9c7f086334f0ef7e0a7b2fde3d64efd7234ea10f1fb6c3b4f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389279} {:answer/id #uuid "ab2665ec-9150-5288-8394-5f9c6a6cd1c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/92/923d1a1b87833551ae6c451f15a6b5d8bdd75623e8d5c20e86357894bc24683d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389280} {:answer/id #uuid "0820dca7-deb5-5697-9159-8f0f4f3fcbc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/81/812c019fe97965e1757b9cf76c18439b69f3d8548b3be1b04530ee0b0e1ad4f4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389281} {:answer/id #uuid "5fbc07a1-e889-568b-82e7-6e63688d1848" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bf/bf2edb811442fc770f6f3c119c06bca3c17d30537f1941448ddb6ab0afa3eb8a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389282} {:answer/id #uuid "ffe48311-c82e-59ca-9751-9099ff7e1583" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/0e/0e766db6832d6c8d2ad052f57ffc4f112ec995bd1462fb6a0998abf72c5d2059.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389283}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5fbc07a1-e889-568b-82e7-6e63688d1848" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bf/bf2edb811442fc770f6f3c119c06bca3c17d30537f1941448ddb6ab0afa3eb8a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389282} :answer-field/id #uuid "34b0aa03-9887-507d-915d-6585c4342069" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389278}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c0feb5e9-8cc6-5750-8eab-e0b55e158194" :question/math-academy-id "q-162241" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams shows all the $5$th roots of unity?" :question/worked-solution "Geometrically, the $5$th roots of unity have the following properties:\n\n- all of them lie on the unit circle centered in the origin,\n\n- one of the roots is $1,$ corresponding to the point $(1,0)$ on the complex plane,\n\n- the roots form a regular pentagon (regular $5$-sided polygon).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n![](images/bf/bf2edb811442fc770f6f3c119c06bca3c17d30537f1941448ddb6ab0afa3eb8a.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281431 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100819 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a32f8ec3-ac69-5b6a-9937-1093556d24d7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a Trigonometric Equation Containing Sine or Cosine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29053ade-ca82-5bab-aa52-fc1416a92dca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278956} {:answer/id #uuid "327f7f81-fa2d-554c-9d59-a0238b6b5c56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279034} {:answer/id #uuid "381d375a-d282-5558-af67-c3f309ae7ea8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279080} {:answer/id #uuid "64e63cbb-8403-5bbf-a00e-fae8cd3dad71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=n\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279509} {:answer/id #uuid "e0ad06ee-b6ca-5ac4-b103-4ef2e8096fc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280579}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0ad06ee-b6ca-5ac4-b103-4ef2e8096fc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280579} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7af4244-b19c-5140-981a-ed78c0638c37" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281275}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d9834690-2d79-5a22-9e52-3ff6dc17940f" :question/math-academy-id "q-16227" :question/problem "What is the general solution to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=1?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(1)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nThe sine function has a period of $2\\pi .$ Hence, the general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389284 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087788 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffddeaa7-a247-54b0-af4b-a29d9bbeee6a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maclaurin Expansion of a Sum of Functions Using Standard Maclaurin Expansions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "124f214f-d111-5270-8f5a-99d12cd7c085" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)=1+2x-2{x}^{2}-\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389286} {:answer/id #uuid "a3f8991a-ae24-560d-aaad-e0f0da3a2a44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)=1+2x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{8{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389287} {:answer/id #uuid "a3ffedca-2ff3-52db-bf69-7683ee3d3b9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)=1+4x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389288} {:answer/id #uuid "2557177c-b9c2-5021-9849-d0b2d282ff54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)=1+2x-2{x}^{2}-\\frac{8{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389289} {:answer/id #uuid "ab5f74b7-8408-5d07-a163-73944c92bf19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)=1+2x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389290}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab5f74b7-8408-5d07-a163-73944c92bf19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)=1+2x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389290} :answer-field/id #uuid "9eadffca-ae05-5f18-bf56-b5c8a1797f1e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389285}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a566bf55-85d0-5a70-9fbb-f45650b09aa0" :question/math-academy-id "q-16230" :question/problem "Find the first four non-zero terms of the Maclaurin expansion of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).$" :question/worked-solution "The Maclaurin expansion of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & =x-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}+\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5!}-⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReplacing $x$ with $2x$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) & =(2x)-\\frac{(2x)^{3}}{3!}+\\frac{(2x)^{5}}{5!}-⋯ \\\\  & =2x-\\frac{{2}^{3}{x}^{3}}{6}+\\frac{{2}^{5}{x}^{5}}{120}-⋯ \\\\  & =2x-\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{4{x}^{5}}{15}-⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe Maclaurin expansion of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4!}-⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the expansions for $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & =(2x-\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{4{x}^{5}}{15}-⋯)+(1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4!}-⋯) \\\\  & =1+2x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389291 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171914 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c2c755c-59e0-5048-b20c-507f13fce441" :knowledge-point/title "Geometric Interpretation of the Roots of Unity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36267af7-3b1c-53e8-8254-4652eb19b61b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/51/51ac4ad5bbc626c58ddbb6efd8c06bb665ef0849591dd846dd959756ea6e41b6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389293} {:answer/id #uuid "ae188fc3-ca39-5ffc-ab80-585fcf2025f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/73/73ac4fabf63cec9e0639bd2213bf2c4df4030ae0c143e0747ebde469418459d6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389294} {:answer/id #uuid "c2b96a31-29a9-5690-9d1f-f4a0e63422a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/71/717710d10e5f9919aa44a9e499509e19790e630027693f3dd72d8b2fd7caee2f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389295} {:answer/id #uuid "a562f990-eae4-516e-aae9-54b81af106e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/40/40af68e4d6e32b333a539fe33dc51ab52a77de3b8db12f930986e468cb033378.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389296} {:answer/id #uuid "b2fc1dee-e469-53cf-9212-2e5f92188e36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ea/ea44776f2632c23a4f52f50282d69492d2948722590ca5dbcaff619ae5079f7e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389297}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36267af7-3b1c-53e8-8254-4652eb19b61b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/51/51ac4ad5bbc626c58ddbb6efd8c06bb665ef0849591dd846dd959756ea6e41b6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389293} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb58a3f9-1543-53b6-8d35-b52ef0687fb6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389292}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "07999dfb-3631-5698-9dcf-dad1b47402f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-162310" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams shows all the $8$th roots of unity?" :question/worked-solution "Geometrically, the $8$th roots of unity have the following properties:\n\n- all of them lie on the unit circle centered in the origin,\n\n- one of the roots is $1,$ corresponding to the point $(1,0)$ on the complex plane,\n\n- the roots form a regular octagon (regular $8$-sided polygon).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n![](images/51/51ac4ad5bbc626c58ddbb6efd8c06bb665ef0849591dd846dd959756ea6e41b6.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389298 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171914 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c2c755c-59e0-5048-b20c-507f13fce441" :knowledge-point/title "Geometric Interpretation of the Roots of Unity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8698d81c-07f1-58ee-9dd6-e41a3eaa29dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/59/59cc6771efc546ca0b6ff4fe7b9fd7a1e7794c20240052c3d8e4e29ad114ec8d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389300} {:answer/id #uuid "07eaee6d-65d6-5afc-9448-c3466e6e1563" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d8/d8a917138beb862f6816fc059847659b1ba11e17d6a7a4906309b021baf1d3e5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389301} {:answer/id #uuid "b0cc1df6-2564-53f0-b5cb-5a4e1d5c3a28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f1/f15f43e3663cfee8328135cb30894c4f268004f4bb74c838cb1bbf2c654c19f9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389302} {:answer/id #uuid "5834d494-4bff-53e7-8745-4b2b513b127b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b4/b4510002c6e424de6ea16f3037237ea9628b0ddd61f7fa4643d412b66b4fd749.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389303} {:answer/id #uuid "1bf18620-f54f-5e1e-8a0a-daf6f078a0ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/05/0525436f0f1ea9283c783bf590ae6dd58408ba5d5e2e6f69fc83af86e470864d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389304}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07eaee6d-65d6-5afc-9448-c3466e6e1563" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d8/d8a917138beb862f6816fc059847659b1ba11e17d6a7a4906309b021baf1d3e5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389301} :answer-field/id #uuid "e17095f8-ba86-50e5-a8b1-f267ffaff592" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389299}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f64f7f30-ad11-543c-8e4a-74267cbdc5a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-162311" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams shows all the $9$th roots of unity?" :question/worked-solution "Geometrically, the $9$th roots of unity have the following properties:\n\n- all of them lie on the unit circle centered in the origin,\n\n- one of the roots is $1,$ corresponding to the point $(1,0)$ on the complex plane,\n\n- the roots form a regular nonagon (regular $9$-sided polygon).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n![](images/d8/d8a917138beb862f6816fc059847659b1ba11e17d6a7a4906309b021baf1d3e5.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281446 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192803 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dab23d8f-dc62-5862-b5e9-e7d5602ad42c" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Root of Unity Using Its Principal Root"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a4e8559-e1f9-5eaf-8f41-2cc950f70eac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279555} {:answer/id #uuid "851cdbdc-0097-5665-9a3b-e276bd7b1aeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279788} {:answer/id #uuid "87e5e19c-1dc1-5fe5-a071-f9fd7d84664a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279805} {:answer/id #uuid "b6c438fb-9a53-510b-b9cc-b42b8c92a314" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280195} {:answer/id #uuid "cd52a6f5-7d83-50ec-a4b5-3722b4271a9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280400}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "87e5e19c-1dc1-5fe5-a071-f9fd7d84664a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279805} :answer-field/id #uuid "17d2ce1b-51be-5bf6-9ddb-b26294e30ce2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280874}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5358c811-fb51-54cc-b39e-4cede46315bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-162343" :question/problem "The $6$th root of unity ${e}^{4\\pi \\text{i}/3}$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{e}^{4\\pi \\text{i}/3}={z}^{p},\n$$\n\nwhere $z$ is the principal $6$th root of unity. What is the value of $p?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the $6$th roots of unity can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}/6}\n$$\n\nwhere $k=0,1,…,5.$ \n\nWe obtain the *principal* $6$th root of unity by substituting $k=1:$\n\n$$\nz={e}^{2\\cdot 1\\cdot \\pi \\text{i}/6}={e}^{\\pi \\text{i}/3}\n$$\n\nWe can write the given root of unity as a power of the principal root as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4\\pi \\text{i}/3} & =({e}^{\\pi \\text{i}/3})^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=4.$"}]}
